course: dictionary
lesson: 993
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Bog has several in this entry, and the words around it show you which one you need. «Bogs and swamps can be dangerous to walk on, so always stay on paths» tells you a bog is wet ground where your feet can sink.
explain: «I need the bog» means something different in a home in Ireland or Britain. Here bog means the toilet, a slang word. The clue is the word «need», and you can tell the meaning by thinking about where you are and what someone is saying.
explain: Bog can also be a verb. «The plan got bogged down in too many details» means it got stuck and could not move on. Test your meaning by putting it in the sentence. If «stuck» fits and still makes sense, you have chosen the right meaning.
fact: A bog is soft, wet, spongy ground, often with moss and peat, where your feet can sink.
fact: Peat is half-rotted dead plants, mostly bog moss, and it grows only about 1 millimetre a year.
fact: Bogs are wetlands, and peatlands store more carbon than all the world's forests together.
fact: Bogs can be deep and dangerous, so always stay on paths and boardwalks.
fact: «Bog-standard» means ordinary, and «bog roll» means toilet paper in everyday British talk.
exercise: «Bogs and swamps can be dangerous to walk on, so always stay on paths.» What is a bog? | *Soft, wet, spongy ground where your feet can sink | A dry, sandy desert | A tall tree growing in salty mud | A toy made of moss
exercise: Ella says, «I need the bog!» in a house in Ireland. What does bog mean here? | A wet, grey day | *The toilet | A marsh full of reeds | A kind of wooden boat
exercise: «The plan got bogged down in too many details.» What does bogged down mean? | *Stuck, so it could not move on | Cooked slowly in a pan | Wrapped up warm in blankets | Bounced high and fast
test: «Peat grows only about 1 millimetre a year.» How fast does peat grow? | About 1 metre a year | About 1 centimetre a day | *About 1 millimetre a year | It does not grow at all
test: Bogs have «preserved» old things, even «bog bodies» thousands of years old. What does preserved most likely mean? | Painted bright | Washed clean | Thrown away | *Kept safe for a very long time
exam: Ben says «The walk crossed a bog» means the walk crossed a toilet. Who is right? | Ben is right: a bog is always a toilet | *Ben is wrong: here a bog is soft, wet ground | Neither meaning fits, so the word does not matter | Both are right
